3 Adversarial nets

The adversarial modeling framework is most straightforward to apply when the models are both multilayer perceptrons. To learn the generator’s distribution p _ { g }over data x, we define a prior on input noise variables p _ { z } ( z ), then represent a mapping to data space as G ( z ; \theta _ { g } ), where G is a differentiable function represented by a multilayer perceptron with parameters \theta _ { g } .. We also define a second multilayer perceptron D ( \pmb { x } ; \theta _ { d } )that outputs a single scalar. D ( \pmb { x } )represents the probability that x came from the data rather than p _ { g } .. We train D to maximize the probability of assigning the correct label to both training examples and samples from G. We simultaneously train G to minimize log ( 1 - D ( G ( z ) ) ). In other words, D and G play the following two-player minimax game with value function V ( \boldsymbol { G } , \boldsymbol { D } ):

Equation: \min _ {G} \max _ {D} V (D, G) = \mathbb {E} _ {\boldsymbol {x} \sim p _ {\text { data }} (\boldsymbol {x})} [ \log D (\boldsymbol {x}) ] + \mathbb {E} _ {\boldsymbol {z} \sim p _ {\boldsymbol {z}} (\boldsymbol {z})} [ \log (1 - D (G (\boldsymbol {z}))) ]. \tag {1}

4.1 Global Optimality of p _ { g } = p _ { \mathbf { d a t a } }

We first consider the optimal discriminator D for any given generator G ..

Proposition 1. For G fixed, the optimal discriminator D is

Equation: D _ {G} ^ {*} (\boldsymbol {x}) = \frac {p _ {\text { data }} (\boldsymbol {x})}{p _ {\text { data }} (\boldsymbol {x}) + p _ {g} (\boldsymbol {x})} \tag {2}

Proof. The training criterion for the discriminator D, given any generator G, is to maximize the quantity V (G, D)

Equation: \begin{array}{l} V (G, D) = \int_ {\pmb {x}} p _ {\mathrm{data}} (\pmb {x}) \log (D (\pmb {x})) d x + \int_ {z} p _ {\pmb {z}} (\pmb {z}) \log (1 - D (g (\pmb {z}))) d z \\ = \int_ {\boldsymbol {x}} p _ {\text { data }} (\boldsymbol {x}) \log (D (\boldsymbol {x})) + p _ {g} (\boldsymbol {x}) \log (1 - D (\boldsymbol {x})) d x \tag {3} \\ \end{array}

For any ( a , b ) \in \mathbb { R } ^ { 2 } \setminus \{ 0 , 0 \}, the function y \to a \log ( y ) + b \log ( 1 - y )achieves its maximum in \begin{array} { r } { [ 0 , 1 ] \mathrm { ~ a t ~ } \frac { \dot { a } } { a + b } . } \end{array}. The discriminator does not need to be defined outside of S u p p ( p _ { \mathrm { d a t a } } ) \cup S u p p ( p _ { g } ), concluding the proof. □

